10. Non-functional requirements [A unless marked]
• Webhook availability: the Go ingest gateway is deployed and scaled separately from the main API, writes to durable storage first, and acknowledges quickly; downstream work runs from queues. Payment events must never be lost, including for suspended tenants.
• Security: TLS everywhere, secrets in a secret manager, argon2id, per-tenant RLS with FORCE, PII redaction in logs, signed URLs for files, rate limits on auth, OTP and public webhooks.
• Data protection: encryption at rest for sensitive fields (R-G08); backups encrypted; residency decision D-10; consent and audit per R-G05/R-G07.
• Performance: grading grid under 2 s on 3G (R-ASM-10); parent pages under 30 KB JSON (R-PAR-03); gate tap-to-result under 1.5 s online (R-GATE-07); webhook handler persists and acknowledges without calling external services.
• Device and network: low-end Android Chrome is the baseline for S2, S3 and S5; installable PWAs; large touch targets (minimum 44 px) in S2 and S5; high-contrast kiosk theme in S5.
• Accessibility: target WCAG 2.1 AA for S1, S3, S4.
• Reliability targets: recovery point objective 15 minutes and recovery time objective 4 hours as starting values; daily backup restore test.
• Observability: structured logs with request_id and tenant_id; metrics for webhook success rate, callback lag, sync backlog, message delivery rates (feeds V-S01 and V-S06).
• Mandatory automated tests: cross-tenant leak test per tenant table; pooled-connection tenant-context test; webhook idempotency and replay test; payment allocation property tests (sum of allocations equals payment; balances never negative); sync conflict tests (offline edits from two devices); consent-gate tests on every outbound path; role-matrix tests from §2; load test of the webhook path before go-live [U: blueprint's Sprint 1 asks for behaviour under load].
11. Paper cross-check results and build order
